---
import Docs from '../layouts/Docs.astro';
import Demo from './Demo.astro';
import release from '../data/release.json';
const card='<section class="sl-container sl-py-12 sl-stack sl-gap-6"><h2 class="sl-text-xl">A responsive card grid</h2><div class="sl-grid sl-md:cols-3 sl-gap-6"><article class="sl-card sl-stack sl-gap-4"><h3 class="sl-text-xl">Plan</h3><p>Start with a clear structure.</p><a class="sl-btn" href="#installation">Read the guide</a></article><article class="sl-card sl-stack sl-gap-4"><h3 class="sl-text-xl">Design</h3><p>Use tokens to connect your choices.</p></article><article class="sl-card sl-stack sl-gap-4"><h3 class="sl-text-xl">Build</h3><p>Ship only the modules you need.</p></article></div></section>';
const button='<link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/buttons.min.css">\n<button class="sl-btn sl-btn--outline">Save changes</button>';
---
<Docs title="Installation, tutorials & releases" description="Install StyloftCSS, build a responsive page, customize tokens and download a versioned CSS release with complete examples." active="guides" section="docs">
<header class="docs-page-header"><h1>From first class to finished page.</h1><p>Practical installation, two original tutorials, complete templates and a measured release you can download.</p></header>
<p class="framework-extension">Looking for a focused walkthrough? <a href="/tutorials/">Read the accordion, responsive header and card-grid tutorials.</a></p>
<nav class="guide-toc" aria-label="Guide sections">{['installation','tutorials','examples','releases','browser-support','accessibility'].map(id=><a href={'#'+id}>{id.replaceAll('-',' ')}</a>)}<a href="/search/#class-reference">Class reference</a></nav>
<section class="guide-section" id="installation"><h2>Install only what you need</h2><p>Download the core CSS and place it in your project’s public CSS directory. No npm package publication or build step is required to use it.</p><a class="sl-btn" href={release.core} download>Download v{release.version} core CSS</a><pre><code>{'<link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/styloft.min.css">'}</code></pre><p>For just buttons, download the standalone buttons pack; it includes its own tokens and dependencies. Use it alone. For several families, use the core plus their optional modules to avoid repeated shared rules.</p><a href="/downloads/components/buttons.min.css" download>Download buttons only</a><pre><code>{button}</code></pre><p>Inside a bundler project, vendor the CSS and import it from your own source path. This repository is currently private for npm publishing; do not assume an npm install command exists. Load your custom CSS after StyloftCSS.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section" id="tutorials"><h2>Tutorial 1: build a responsive card section</h2><p>Start with sl-container for readable width, sl-stack for vertical rhythm, and sl-grid for columns. The sl-md:cols-3 class adds three columns at the core medium breakpoint. Smaller screens keep the natural single-column layout.</p><Demo title="Responsive cards" description="Edit the HTML to add another card or adjust the gap utilities." html={card}/><h3>Tutorial 2: theme with design tokens</h3><p>Override semantic custom properties in your application stylesheet, after the core. Keep foreground and background pairs together and check contrast. A data-theme attribute scopes the built-in light or dark palette.</p><pre><code>{':root {\n  --sl-accent: #006c7a;\n  --sl-on-accent: #ffffff;\n  --sl-radius: .75rem;\n}\n\n/* Apply to the page root: <html data-theme="dark"> */'}</code></pre><p>Optional modules inherit these tokens. Consult <a href="/themes/">tokens and themes</a> for visual examples, and <a href="/search/">search the class reference</a> for each class’s real declarations and source modules.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section" id="examples"><h2>Complete page examples</h2><p>These templates include document metadata, a skip link, responsive layout and semantic landmarks. They use only the framework CSS and no component JavaScript.</p><ul><li><a href={release.examples+'landing.html'}>Open the studio landing page</a> — header, hero, service cards and footer.</li><li><a href={release.examples+'contact.html'}>Open the contact template</a> — labels, native field constraints and form layout. Submission is deliberately disabled until you connect a backend.</li></ul><p>Download the archive below to get both HTML files with the stylesheet. Replace sample copy and contact details before publishing.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section" id="releases"><h2>Version {release.version} — release candidate</h2><p>The core measures 3,313 bytes gzip, excluding optional modules and website assets. This archive contains the CSS distributions, standalone packs, MIT license and complete examples. It has not been published to npm or as a GitHub release.</p><p><a class="sl-btn" href={release.archive} download>Download v{release.version} ZIP</a> <a href={release.checksum} download>SHA-256 checksum</a></p><details class="sl-my-6"><summary>Verify this download</summary><pre><code>{'Get-FileHash .\\'+release.archive.split('/').at(-1)+' -Algorithm SHA256'}</code></pre><p>Expected SHA-256: <code>{release.sha256}</code></p></details><h3>Changelog</h3><ul><li>CSS core with tokens, layers, layout utilities and basic components.</li><li>Optional modules, 41 standalone packs and native interaction examples.</li><li>Dark/light documentation, HTML playgrounds, class search and complete page templates.</li></ul><p>Pin versioned download paths for reproducible projects. Once an official release is tagged, its archive must not be replaced; new changes need a new version.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section" id="browser-support"><h2>Browser support and fallbacks</h2><p>The core targets current Chrome/Edge, Firefox and Safari with CSS custom properties and cascade layers. Native dialog invoker commands, popovers, scroll-driven animation, customizable selects and smooth details closing have different support levels. Basic content and native fields remain usable when visual enhancements are unavailable.</p><p>Validation runs in Chromium; Firefox, Safari and assistive-technology verification are not yet claimed. Read each component’s notes before relying on newer features. Sliders use native scrolling; calendars and color areas explicitly identify the application logic they need.</p></section>
<section class="guide-section" id="accessibility"><h2>Build for keyboard and assistive technology</h2><p>Use meaningful headings, labelled controls and visible link names. Preserve the skip link and focus indicator. Keep the native details, dialog and form semantics shown in the examples; do not add ARIA roles without implementing their required behavior.</p><p>Motion respects prefers-reduced-motion. Check your own token combinations for text and control contrast, and test at narrow widths and with enlarged text. Automated checks supplement keyboard testing; they do not certify WCAG conformance or replace a screen-reader review.</p></section>
</Docs>
